<!--
    GH-1034, P2 switch model, option switchrunon (default on).

    pick dispatches on a byte with no bounds check, the shape a compiler
    emits when it knows the selector is in range (Rust matching on a u8 enum
    parameter): movzbl, lea table, movslq, add, jmp. Its own table has four
    rows (cases returning 0x11..0x14). The 252 rows after them, which the
    basic model reads because a byte allows 256 values, land in the bodies of
    the next two functions, left and right, the way the next functions'
    tables do in a linked Rust binary.

    Source (GNU as, linked at 0x100000): pick at 0x100000, left at 0x100100,
    right at 0x100200, the table at 0x100300 (4 own rows, then 126 pairs of
    rows into left and right).

    Pass 1 (default): the table stops at its four rows, so pick prints four
        cases and none of left's or right's code (#1, #2, and the single
        counts of #3-#5).
    Pass 2 (option switchrunon off): the whole-width table is followed into
        left and right, whose returns print as cases of pick up to 0xff
        (#3-#5).

    pick2 (0x100800) masks its byte to eight rows: four own cases returning
    0x41..0x44, a fifth returning 0x45 from pick2.cold at 0x100780, below
    pick2's entry where gcc places a function's split-off cold part, then
    rows into left2 and right2. The first row out lands below the entry, so
    the default pass keeps the whole table and the cold case (#6, #7).
-->
